#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5eea139-b136-5137-9fab-3b6ae9daa7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5eea139-b136-5137-9fab-3b6ae9daa7f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317962} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dac418b-8090-533a-9b21-0d06af629ce3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e452deae-529e-5f25-ac44-d802af087cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10+e^{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e452deae-529e-5f25-ac44-d802af087cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10+e^{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317964} :answer-field/id #uuid "efce137b-7783-5586-bc62-1c3223128b09" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1660d2d6-cfef-5eb0-bb4e-3e8543059f52" :question/math-academy-id "q-278520" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-10)-6\\ge 0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-10)-6 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-10) & \\ge 6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-10) & \\ge 14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-10)} & \\ge {e}^{14} \\\\ x-10 & \\ge {e}^{14} \\\\ x & \\ge {e}^{14}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-10>0\\,⇒\\,x>10\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge {e}^{14}+10,$ which can be expressed as $[{e}^{14}+10,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>10,$ which can be expressed as $(10,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[{e}^{14}+10,\\infty )\\cap (10,\\infty )=[{e}^{14}+10,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [{e}^{14}+10,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge {e}^{14}+10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f90360a-6b04-56ac-ba8f-f870ce198097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f90360a-6b04-56ac-ba8f-f870ce198097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317967} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf333ee9-41b7-51a6-b8f4-237322a0a13d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317966} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a04dce82-e42c-58ec-bbe2-80e41dfab12b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2e+4}{e-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a04dce82-e42c-58ec-bbe2-80e41dfab12b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2e+4}{e-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317969} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eda7b92-309b-5a67-9183-210c1ce5c29c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a45f16c4-78c7-510b-9bcb-5c1470f6997a" :question/math-academy-id "q-278753" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)}\\cdot {e}^{-1} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ (2x+4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{e} & <x-2 \\\\ 2x+4 & <xe-2e \\\\ 4+2e & <xe-2x \\\\ 4+2e & <x(e-2) \\\\ x & >\\frac{4+2e}{e-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-2 \\\\ x-2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-2$ and $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty )$ and $(2,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty )\\cap (-2,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\nx>\\frac{4+2e}{e-2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21bd3d0-3477-5d50-804d-aa2419d09d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1-2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317972} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5493e9-9692-5ac1-b6a3-bab54b134ee8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{1+{e}^{6}}{2{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317973} {:answer/id #uuid "659b3f4d-f3b6-5d39-b8b3-d9a6b083fbec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317974} {:answer/id #uuid "400fee51-77c2-5f10-b1b8-da4f315a231f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x\\le \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317975} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae1218f-3c76-5f23-8ce7-822637fd36cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{2+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "659b3f4d-f3b6-5d39-b8b3-d9a6b083fbec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317974} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f204b0-39df-5f95-b4f4-bd667bb80636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fecd844-b3f2-5227-a231-459b5c517f86" :question/math-academy-id "q-88006" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+4\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)+4 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & \\ge -4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) & \\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} & \\ge {e}^{-6} \\\\ x-2 & \\ge {e}^{-6} \\\\ x & \\ge {e}^{-6}+2 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-2>0\\,⇒\\,x>2\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=[\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge \\frac{1+2{e}^{6}}{{e}^{6}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af50a08-ffd1-5956-81e4-2e871a9ea4d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<\\sqrt[3]{7}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317979} {:answer/id #uuid "23485f21-2a4c-50f4-8a27-ed6ef47f314a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<x<\\sqrt[3]{7}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317980} {:answer/id #uuid "246e9919-43e2-5975-beb9-b62f17f3e55c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\sqrt[3]{7}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317981} {:answer/id #uuid "29ce4498-6c7f-58df-822a-9a5b9367b71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\sqrt[3]{7}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317982} {:answer/id #uuid "5706d665-4907-5e10-8e33-d1f264d7c1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\sqrt[3]{7}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29ce4498-6c7f-58df-822a-9a5b9367b71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\sqrt[3]{7}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317982} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c9ffd9-9054-59d2-a7ce-8be7fd3ec3b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e16eafea-1353-5084-aabe-5cac6210b9da" :question/math-academy-id "q-88016" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)-\\frac{4}{3}>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)-\\frac{4}{3} & >0 \\\\ 4{log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & >\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & >\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $7.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)} & >{7}^{1/3} \\\\ x+1 & >\\sqrt[3]{7} \\\\ x & >\\sqrt[3]{7}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+1>0\\,⇒\\,x>-1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\sqrt[3]{7}-1,$ which can be expressed as $(\\sqrt[3]{7}-1,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[3]{7}-1,\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(\\sqrt[3]{7}-1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\sqrt[3]{7}-1,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>\\sqrt[3]{7}-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6787a43-c1b8-549b-9a9f-da94c1b2941f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317986} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a909a4-435c-5805-9d07-6568f933f10e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1+\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317987} {:answer/id #uuid "eedd13d0-10a0-5dad-9311-296fa34ba6e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1}\\le x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317988} {:answer/id #uuid "d902f5d3-87c7-5373-bf95-bbb3d528f00b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1}\\le x<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317989} {:answer/id #uuid "43f8630a-cc8e-5529-aacd-2a13cd1d1aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 5{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6787a43-c1b8-549b-9a9f-da94c1b2941f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317986} :answer-field/id #uuid "96521342-7263-5a5b-91a6-3515a8418fba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5501715a-4365-5698-82ff-b02c4c1ee58b" :question/math-academy-id "q-88037" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)-2\\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)-2} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}\\cdot {e}^{-2} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)} \\\\ (x-1){e}^{-2} & \\ge 5x \\\\ x-1 & \\ge 5{e}^{2}x \\\\ -1 & \\ge (5{e}^{2}-1)x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1} & \\ge x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >1 \\\\ 5x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le -\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1}].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>1$ and $x>0,$ which can be expressed as $(1,\\infty )$ and $(0,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{5{e}^{2}-1}]\\cap (1,\\infty )\\cap (0,\\infty )=∅\n$$\n\nThe intersection is empty. Therefore, there is no solution."}]}
